## 4. UI Automation Guidelines

### 4.1 Page Object Model Design

- Create one page object per logical page or significant component
- Keep locators private within page objects — never expose them to tests
- Page methods should represent user actions, not element interactions
- Return page objects from actions to enable method chaining (fluent interface)
- Implement `is_loaded()` or similar verification methods for each page
- Separate locator definitions from behavior — consider locator files or constants

### 4.2 Locator Strategy

- Prefer stable locators in this order: data-testid attributes, IDs, name attributes, CSS selectors, XPath (last resort)
- Collaborate with developers to add data-testid attributes for test stability
- Avoid locators dependent on text content — these break with internationalization
- Never use absolute XPath or positional selectors
- Document locator conventions and enforce through code review

### 4.3 Wait Strategies

- Never use hard-coded sleeps — always use explicit waits with conditions
- Create custom wait conditions for complex scenarios
- Set reasonable default timeouts with override capability
- Implement retry mechanisms for known flaky interactions
- Log wait conditions for debugging slow tests

### 4.4 Browser Management

- Support multiple browsers through configuration, not code changes
- Use browser factories for consistent initialization
- Implement proper cleanup — close browsers even on test failure
- Support headless mode for CI/CD execution
- Configure appropriate window sizes for consistent rendering
- Clear browser state between tests: cookies, storage, cache

### 4.5 Screenshot and Evidence Capture

- Capture screenshots automatically on test failure
- Take screenshots at key checkpoints for visual verification
- Include page source/DOM on failure for debugging
- Implement video recording capability for complex flows
- Organize evidence by test run and test name

