## 5. API/Backend Automation Guidelines

### 5.1 API Client Design

- Create typed client classes wrapping HTTP operations
- Implement request/response logging for all API calls
- Use session management for authenticated endpoints
- Support multiple serialization formats: JSON, XML, form data
- Handle rate limiting and retry logic at the client level
- Validate response schemas, not just status codes

### 5.2 Request Building

- Use builder patterns for complex request construction
- Externalize request templates and payloads
- Support dynamic value substitution in templates
- Implement request signing/authentication as middleware
- Log request details before execution for debugging

### 5.3 Response Validation

- Validate HTTP status codes as the first assertion
- Use JSON schema validation for response structure
- Assert on specific field values for business logic
- Implement custom matchers for complex validations
- Compare responses against expected baselines where appropriate
- Check response headers when relevant (caching, content-type, etc.)

### 5.4 API Test Independence

- Each test should set up its own prerequisites via API calls
- Clean up created resources after test completion
- Use unique identifiers to prevent test data collision
- Mock external dependencies when testing internal services
- Implement idempotent test setup where possible

### 5.5 Contract Testing

- Validate API contracts between services
- Compare current responses against stored contracts
- Alert on breaking changes in API structure
- Version contracts alongside API versions
- Integrate contract validation into CI pipeline

