## 6. ETL Automation Guidelines

### 6.1 Data Validation Strategy

- Validate record counts at source, transformation, and destination
- Implement checksum validation for data integrity
- Compare aggregations between source and target
- Validate data types and format transformations
- Check for duplicate records and orphaned data
- Verify referential integrity across related tables

### 6.2 Schema Validation

- Validate target schema matches expected structure before loading
- Check column data types, nullability, and constraints
- Verify index and key definitions
- Compare schemas across environments for consistency
- Detect and alert on schema drift

### 6.3 Transformation Testing

- Test each transformation rule in isolation
- Validate business logic transformations with known input/output pairs
- Test boundary conditions and edge cases in transformations
- Verify handling of null values and special characters
- Test date/time transformations across time zones

### 6.4 Data Quality Checks

- Implement completeness checks — all expected data is present
- Validate accuracy — transformed data matches business rules
- Check consistency — same data produces same results
- Verify timeliness — data is processed within expected windows
- Monitor uniqueness — no unintended duplicates

### 6.5 Performance and Volume Testing

- Establish baseline performance metrics for ETL jobs
- Test with production-like data volumes
- Monitor resource utilization during execution
- Set thresholds for acceptable processing times
- Alert on performance degradation trends

### 6.6 Database Connection Management

- Use connection pooling for efficiency
- Implement connection timeout and retry logic
- Support multiple database types through abstraction
- Never hardcode connection strings — use secure configuration
- Close connections explicitly in teardown

