## 8. GitLab CI/CD Pipeline Integration

### 8.1 Pipeline Design Principles

- Design pipelines to be triggered manually or automatically
- Support selective test execution based on variables
- Implement parallel execution for faster feedback
- Use pipeline stages logically: setup, test, report, cleanup
- Cache dependencies to reduce execution time
- Support both branch pipelines and merge request pipelines

### 8.2 Variable Categories

**Environment Configuration:**
Variables that define where tests run — set at project or group level for each environment.

**Execution Control:**
Variables that control how tests run — typically modified per pipeline run.

**Credentials and Secrets:**
Sensitive variables — always masked and protected, set at project level, never in code.

**Feature Flags:**
Variables that enable/disable specific behaviors — useful for gradual rollout or debugging.

### 8.3 Pipeline Execution Guidelines

- Always specify target environment — pipeline should fail if not provided
- Use smoke test suite for quick validation on every commit
- Run regression suite on scheduled pipelines or before releases
- Disable headless mode only when debugging UI tests locally
- Increase parallel workers for faster execution, decrease if hitting resource limits
- Set retry count to zero when debugging to see failures immediately

### 8.4 Artifact and Report Management

- Generate test reports as pipeline artifacts
- Configure artifact expiration based on needs (7-30 days typical)
- Publish reports to GitLab Pages or external hosting
- Archive test evidence (screenshots, logs) with reports
- Implement report history for trend tracking

### 8.5 Pipeline Security

- Never log sensitive variables — use GitLab's masking feature
- Restrict who can run pipelines against production
- Use protected variables for production credentials
- Implement approval gates for sensitive environment testing
- Audit pipeline execution history

### 8.6 Notifications and Alerting

- Configure notifications for pipeline failures
- Send summary reports to relevant channels (Slack, email)
- Include failure details and links to reports in notifications
- Differentiate notification urgency based on branch/environment
- Implement escalation for repeated failures

